  [38;5;153mnormalizeLoginResponse(raw)[39m is the only function that knows this shape — it maps the wire format to [38;5;153m{ ok, userId, [39m
  [38;5;153mdisplayName, error }[39m and treats a missing or non-string [38;5;153muserId[39m on an [38;5;153mok[39m response as a failure rather than storing
  [38;5;153mundefined[39m. When the real backend lands, that one function is the change.

  [1mSession store[0m

  Key [38;5;153mapp.session.v1[39m, versioned so a later shape change can't read stale garbage. [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m is guarded: corrupt value
  is treated as no session and cleared rather than thrown. [38;5;153mcreateSessionStore(storage = localStorage)[39m returns [38;5;153m{ [39m
  [38;5;153mgetUserId, getSession, setSession, clearSession }[39m; the default instance is exported for app use, and tests pass a
  plain object as [38;5;153mstorage[39m.

  [38;5;153mclearSession()[39m exists and nothing calls it — per your scope call. Worth restating plainly: [1monce someone logs in, that
[0m  [1muserId stays in their browser permanently.[0m There's no way to clear it short of devtools or clearing site data.

  [1mSecurity boundary, recorded in the spec[0m

  The stored [38;5;153muserId[39m is a display and correlation value only. It is never an authorization fact — any later form that
  gates behavior on it is trivially defeated by editing one string in devtools. The password is never stored and never
  logged; the existing [38;5;153mconsole.log("Logging in:", username)[39m keeps logging only the username.

  [1mTesting[0m

  [38;5;153mnode:test[39m and [38;5;153mnode:assert[39m — built in, no dependencies. Coverage: session store round-trip, corrupt-JSON recovery,
  clear; and [38;5;153mnormalizeLoginResponse[39m against success, declared failure, malformed, and [38;5;153mok[39m-without-[38;5;153muserId[39m.

  This repo has no linter, formatter, or test runner configured, so it's cheapest to decide now.
